WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220
do more productive efforts, specifically focused on third party's spend, which is upwards

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.740
of $10 billion for the company, and that is what we continue to do.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:16.180
As we look at Q2, we actually see while gross margin, quarter-on-quarter gross margin has

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.840
been a negative, but SG&A as a percentage of sales has actually been a positive, negating

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.380
that gross margin impact.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:31.460
And that, I would assume, I would think is, from a prognosis perspective, that is something

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.040
that we will continue towards the second half of the year.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.460
Thank you.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:37.560
Thank you.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:43.040
Your next question comes from the line of James Quigley from Goldman Sachs.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:43.940
Please go ahead.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:45.420
Great.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:46.260
Thank you for my questions.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.300
I've got one on deals and M&As.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.860
I think, Vaz, your quote on Bloomberg is you'd consider larger deals again.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:58.660
So this is a bit of a transition into the commentary over the years.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.900
A few years back, it was no big deals.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.540
Then it was focused on bolt-ons.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.020
Then we had avidity, which is large.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.820
Later stage and a bit larger.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:12.760
So what's changed, either internally at Novartis or externally, that's driven openness to larger

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.080
deals?

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.780
And what could a bigger deal look like in terms of strategic fit, given your therapeutic

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.900
areas and technology set?

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:20.560
Thank you.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:22.060
Yeah, thanks, James.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.100
I don't know what exactly Bloomberg wrote, but I can say there's no change in our M&A

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:26.580
strategy.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:33.120
We've been, I think, disciplined and consistent that we focus on steady deals.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.300
We've done a lot of small deals in the kind of sub-$2 billion range, which with the

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:45.500
upfronts in that range, often lower, and then selectively do larger deals in the range

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:53.300
of things like avidity when there is a compelling asset that fits with either our platform strategy

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:57.440
or our TA strategy or both, and avidity fit both.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.300
So no change at all in our M&A strategy.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.880
We don't need to do anything larger than that.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:09.180
We have full confidence in our internal portfolio and pipeline and our R&D engine, and yet we

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.200
know we need to constantly supplement that engine with additional external innovation.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.360
So you can expect just a continuation of what you've seen over the recent years.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:18.820
Great.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:19.160
Thank you.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.120
Thank you.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.660
Your next question comes from the line of Simon Baker from Roftal & Co.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.600
Please go ahead.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.100
Thank you for taking my question.

